# modules/image/checks-coverage.rules - the container-image module's own
# per-owner registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `checks-<name>.rules` row,
# docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) for the two general acquisition/enumeration
# coverage checks IMG-06 adds: `IMAGE-COV-UNKNOWN_DISTRO-01` and
# `IMAGE-COV-LAYER_UNREADABLE-01`.
#
# A SEPARATE FILE FROM checks-advisories.rules, deliberately, even though
# both are `script: run.sh` module-level reductions and both land in this
# same ticket: checks-advisories.rules is scoped to the advisory-DB-ecosystem
# family (`IMAGE-COV-NO_ADVISORY_DB-01`), while these two are about whether
# this module could read the image's OWN metadata at all - a distinct
# semantic owner, and the "every ticket writes
# its OWN checks-<name>.rules" binding rule is about avoiding an add/add conflict class,
# not about literally one file per ticket; splitting by semantic family here
# costs nothing and keeps a future dpkg/rpm-scoped coverage check
# (IMG-07/IMG-12) from having any reason to touch this file either.
#
# `script: run.sh` because both fire from the module's own driver
# (modules/image/run.sh, via modules/image/engine.sh's
# image_report_unknown_distro/image_report_layer_unreadable) rather than
# from a distro-specific script - IMAGE-COV-LAYER_UNREADABLE-01 in
# particular can fire before any distro/ecosystem is even resolved.
#
# `tags: passive` / `tags: quick` - both checks read already-extracted local
# metadata (an apk database path that turned out absent, or a collect
# result naming what could not be read) with no network call and no
# mutation, mirroring modules/image/checks-advisories.rules' own tags for
# the identical reason.
#
# `coverage-scope: image-id` - the only legal value for the `IMAGE` module
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's frozen table).

id: IMAGE-COV-UNKNOWN_DISTRO-01
title: Container image scanning did NOT run - no recognised package database in any layer of this image
script: run.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: none
owasp: none
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: This image's own /etc/os-release names a resolved distro
  release, but no apk/dpkg/rpm package database exists in any layer -
  typically a distroless or scratch-based final build stage. If this image
  really does ship packages managed by one of those tools, check whether
  the final build stage strips the package manager's own metadata
  directory. Until it is present, this run says NOTHING about this image's
  installed packages.

id: IMAGE-COV-LAYER_UNREADABLE-01
title: Container image scanning is incomplete - one or more layers or members could not be read
script: run.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: none
owasp: none
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Re-save or re-export this image and re-scan. If the problem
  persists, the archive may be corrupt or a layer member may be malformed -
  see this finding's own evidence for the specific path(s) and refusal
  reason(s).
